<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<NamedCredential xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        🔴 allowMergeFieldsInBody MUST be true. THIS IS THE ONE LINE THAT MAKES THE FEATURE WORK,
        AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE OTHER TWO NAMED CREDENTIALS IN THIS REPO.

        OpenAI_API and SharePoint both set it FALSE, because both authenticate by HTTP header.
        Cloudflare Turnstile's /turnstile/v0/siteverify authenticates by a FORM FIELD IN THE POST
        BODY (secret=<secret>&response=<token>). TurnstileVerificationService therefore writes
        {!$Credential.Turnstile_Credential.Secret_Key} into the request body, and the platform only
        substitutes a $Credential merge field inside a body when this flag is true. With it false
        the literal string "{!$Credential.Turnstile_Credential.Secret_Key}" is transmitted as the
        secret and Cloudflare answers {"success":false,"error-codes":["invalid-input-secret"]} —
        a 200 OK, not an error, so nothing throws and every submission is refused with a
        user-facing "could not verify" message that no log explains.

        🔴 THAT FAILURE MODE IS INVISIBLE TO A DEPLOY. This file deploys green with the flag set
        either way. The only proof the merge field resolves is a live siteverify call: a
        deliberately invalid token must come back "invalid-input-response" (secret accepted,
        token rejected) and NOT "invalid-input-secret" (secret never resolved). That probe was run
        against this org before this file was wired into BrokerPortalService; the result is
        recorded in ARCHITECTURE.md §3.5 and in TurnstileVerificationService's class header.
        Anyone changing this line must re-run it.

        generateAuthorizationHeader is false, matching OpenAI_API and for the same reason in
        reverse: there is no Authorization header to generate here at all. Turnstile's endpoint is
        unauthenticated at the transport layer; the secret in the body IS the authentication.
        Setting it true would attach a header Cloudflare ignores and would serve no purpose.

        allowMergeFieldsInHeader is false — nothing merges into a header on this callout, and
        leaving it true would widen the surface on which a credential value can leak into a header
        that some future code path logs.
    -->
    <allowMergeFieldsInBody>true</allowMergeFieldsInBody>
    <allowMergeFieldsInHeader>false</allowMergeFieldsInHeader>
    <calloutStatus>Enabled</calloutStatus>
    <generateAuthorizationHeader>false</generateAuthorizationHeader>
    <label>Cloudflare Turnstile</label>
    <namedCredentialParameters>
        <parameterName>Url</parameterName>
        <parameterType>Url</parameterType>
        <parameterValue>https://challenges.cloudflare.com</parameterValue>
    </namedCredentialParameters>
    <namedCredentialParameters>
        <externalCredential>Turnstile_Credential</externalCredential>
        <parameterName>ExternalCredential</parameterName>
        <parameterType>Authentication</parameterType>
    </namedCredentialParameters>
    <namedCredentialType>SecuredEndpoint</namedCredentialType>
</NamedCredential>
